WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.238
The Moon does something strange.

00:00:02.238 --> 00:00:07.344
It rotates on its axis at exactly the same rate that it orbits the Earth.

00:00:07.344 --> 00:00:10.702
That means one side of the Moon always faces us.

00:00:10.702 --> 00:00:14.829
The other side — the far side — is permanently turned away.

00:00:14.829 --> 00:00:19.445
No human eye saw the far side of the Moon until October 7th, 1959.

00:00:19.445 --> 00:00:23.292
Luna 3, a Soviet probe, returned the first photographs.

00:00:23.292 --> 00:00:24.481
They were grainy.

00:00:24.481 --> 00:00:25.391
Low contrast.

00:00:25.391 --> 00:00:30.846
And they showed something nobody had predicted: the far side looks different .

00:00:30.846 --> 00:00:35.533
Fewer of the dark plains — the maria — that dominate the near side.

00:00:35.533 --> 00:00:36.512
More cratered.

00:00:36.512 --> 00:00:36.932
Older.

00:00:36.932 --> 00:00:37.491
Rougher.

00:00:37.491 --> 00:00:39.030
No one has been there.

00:00:39.030 --> 00:00:41.618
Twelve people have stood on the Moon.

00:00:41.618 --> 00:00:43.996
All twelve stood on the near side.

00:00:43.996 --> 00:00:52.040
You can rotate this map and see the far side, but until the late 2010s, almost no spacecraft had even landed there.

00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:59.525
Then, in January 2019, Chang'e 4 made the first soft landing on the far side, inside the Von Kármán crater.

00:00:59.525 --> 00:01:11.136
To do this, China first had to launch a separate spacecraft — Queqiao , the magpie bridge — into a halo orbit around the Earth-Moon L2 Lagrange point beyond the Moon.

00:01:11.136 --> 00:01:12.605
Queqiao is the relay.

00:01:12.605 --> 00:01:17.151
Without it, a lander on the far side has no way to talk to Earth.

00:01:17.151 --> 00:01:18.760
The Moon is in the way.

00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:24.216
The Yutu-2 rover has been driving inside Von Kármán crater since that landing.

00:01:24.216 --> 00:01:25.335
It moves slowly.

00:01:25.335 --> 00:01:28.413
Sleeps through the fourteen-day lunar night.

00:01:28.413 --> 00:01:30.371
Wakes to study the regolith.

00:01:30.371 --> 00:01:33.099
Reports home through the magpie bridge.

00:01:33.099 --> 00:01:35.897
Most of the far side is still untouched.

00:01:35.897 --> 00:01:44.430
When the next crewed mission goes to the Moon, it will probably land on the near side — Artemis has chosen the south pole.

00:01:44.430 --> 00:01:49.956
Sometime after that, somebody will set foot on the far side for the first time.

00:01:49.956 --> 00:01:52.824
Imagine the photograph they'll send back.

00:01:52.824 --> 00:01:55.272
Look at this map and pick the spot.

